s `rows`, then `totalBytes`, `medianBytes` and `maxBytes`. Each entry of `rows` carries `id`, `bytes` and `estimatedTokens`, where bytes are the UTF-8 length of `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` and tokens divide that by four, the renderer's own characters per token.\n99: - Sandbox: the run makes one `ssa-cost-` directory under the OS temp dir and sets `SPECKIT_IPC_SOCKET_DIR` to it for every hook process, so the operator's warm daemon is never reached. At the end it stops only a process whose command line names that directory and removes it.\n100: \n101: ---\n102: \n103: ## 4. STRUCTURE\n104: \n105: | Path | Purpose |\n106: |---|---|\n107: | `gate3-corpus-runner.mjs` | MJS source file in this folder. |\n108: | `score-routing-corpus.py` | PY source file in this folder. |\n109: | `command-prompts.jsonl` | Prompt corpus with a gold slash command per row, covering every tracked command. |\n110: | `capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs` | Scores `command-prompts.jsonl` under the pinned regime and prints or writes the baseline. Its `attachedMetrics` key holds own-row, attached and combined plain reach per split and per command family, attached false offers, the typed rows where a command and its owner skill read as ambiguous, and the command attached to each row. An attached hit counts only on a row the ranked bridge did not already land. The optional `--real-embedding` flag scores the same corpus against this machine's own skill graph database and live embedder in place of the fixture vectors and word overlap the default run substitutes, so its numbers describe an environment rather than a reproducible projection. It refuses `--write` because the committed baseline comes only from the fixture regime. Before scoring it proves two things, that a live embedder answers and serves the `nomic-embed-text` model family the stored vectors were built with, and that the skill graph database holds a populated `vec_cmd_*` command vector table. A missing piece exits 2 with a message that names it. Each row in its `rows` output also carries `offered`, which is true when any of the top three recommendations maps to a command bridge, so it measures reach rather than whether a brief head offered the command. |\n111: | `score-command-transcripts.mjs` | Scores captured sessions through the shared session readers. `--summary <run-log> [<run-log>...]` scores every row one or more run logs list, per runtime and arm, and `--runtime <name> --locator '<json>'` scores the one session a locator names. `--row-id <id>` narrows either mode to one row and `--json` prints machine-readable output. Each per-arm block reports `startRate`, `cappedStartRows`, `offered`, `cappedOfferRows`, `startedOfOffered`, `offerReached`, `deliveryUnknown`, `offerSeen`, `offerDeclined`, `offerFieldAbsent`, `unsafe`, `armIncomplete` and `byPolicy`, where `byPolicy` holds a `run`, `start` and `ask` tally of rows, capped rows, started rows and rows that wrote. `cappedOfferRows` counts offers whose turn hit its cap, `offerReached` counts offers on a turn whose brief arrived, `deliveryUnknown` counts rows whose brief state is `unknown`, `offerSeen` and `offerDeclined` read the session's `COMMAND:` lines, and `offerFieldAbsent` counts rows that carry no offer field. `unsafe` also counts a workflow loop that started without its init, and a Claude turn that keeps only the `<command-name>` tag of a typed command counts as a start because Claude Code expands that command before the model sees it. The text output prints one `policy` line per policy under each arm. Exit 2 means nothing was scored: bad flags, a missing or malformed run log (`run_log_missing`, `run_log_malformed`), or a reader refusal printed with its own code such as `capability_gated` or `locator_unavailable`. |\n112: | `command-offer.mjs` | Recovers the attribution fields for a run line. `parseOffer` reads the command and policy out of one head, `replayOffer` replays a checkout's Claude prompt hook after the turn, `briefDelivered` reads the session record, and `backfillRunLog` writes the fields onto a finished log with a refusal code for a holdout, unknown or same-path input. |\n113: | `run-command-behaviour.mjs` | Runs one live turn per row and records the locator, exit and changed paths. `--print-argv <runtime>` prints a runtime's argv, `--list <set>` prints a set's row ids, and a run takes `--runtime` with `--set` or repeated `--row-id`, `--out <run-log>`, optional `--arm`, `--fixture` and `--dry-run`. `--backfill <run-log> --out <new-log> [--brief-only]` rewrites a finished log with `offeredCommand`, `offeredPolicy` and `briefDelivered` instead of running a turn, and prints a JSON refusal with `same_path`, `holdout_row` or `unknown_row` when it cannot. After each turn it restores the checkout to HEAD and reports any `leftoverPaths` whose status still differs from the snapshot taken before the turn. Exit 2 refuses instead of running: bad flags, an unknown runtime or set, a holdout or unknown row, a set with fewer rows than it needs (`set_short`), a dirty worktree outside the run log's folder (`worktree_dirty`), a checkout with a git remote (`remote_present`), or a Codex arm whose project hook is unapproved (`codex-hooks-unapproved`). |\n114: | `command-routing-baseline.json` | Captured command routing numbers that `tests/command-routing-slice.vitest.ts` holds the scorer to. |\n115: \n116: ---\n117: \n118: ## 5. CONFIGURATION\n119: \n120: | Setting | Default | Purpose |\n121: |---|---|---|\n122: | sk-code surface | OPENCODE | Applies OpenCode TypeScript, JavaScript, Python, Shell and config conventions. |\n123: | README scope | Direct folder | This file documents this folder, not sibling folders. |\n124: \n125: ---\n126: \n127: ## 6. USAGE EXAMPLES\n128: \n129: **Audit this folder**\n130: \n131: ```text\n132: User request: Check .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy for sk-code and README coverage.\n133: Skill routing: sk-code plus sk-doc.\n134: Expected output: Findings recorded in the 026 audit report.\n135: ```\n136: \n137: **Score captured runs**\n138: \n139: ```bash\n140: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/score-command-transcripts.mjs \\\n141:   --summary scratch/runs-pre.jsonl scratch/runs-post.jsonl --json\n142: ```\n143: \n144: Exit 2 means no score was produced. A missing or malformed log prints `run_log_missing` or `run_log_malformed`, and a session the reader cannot open prints its own code, such as `capability_gated`.\n145: \n146: **Inspect and run one behaviour turn**\n147: \n148: ```bash\n149: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs --print-argv claude\n150: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs \\\n151:   --runtime claude --set chat --out scratch/runs-pre.jsonl --dry-run\n152: ```\n153: \n154: Exit 2 means the run was refused before or during its first prompt: bad flags, an unknown runtime or set, a holdout row, a dirty worktree outside the run log's folder, or a checkout that still has a git remote.\n155: \n156: **Backfill a finished log**\n157: \n158: ```bash\n159: node .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/run-command-behaviour.mjs \\\n160:   --backfill scratch/runs-pre.jsonl --out scratch/runs-pre-attributed.jsonl\n161: ```\n162: \n163: The output log holds the same lines plus `offeredCommand`, `offeredPolicy`, `offerError`, `briefDelivered` and `offerBackfilled`. Exit 2 means the output was refused, and the printed JSON names why: `same_path`, `holdout_row`, `unknown_row` or a missing `--out`.\n164: \n165: ---\n166: \n167: ## 7. TROUBLESHOOTING\n168: \n169: | What You See | Cause | Fix |\n170: |---|---|---|\n171: | README appears stale | Source files changed after this audit | Refresh the structure table and rerun the 026 audit check. |\n172: | Verification command is unclear | Folder is a helper boundary | Use the nearest package or skill-level verification command. |\n173: \n174: ---\n175: \n176: ## 8. RELATED DOCUMENTS\n177: \n178: | Document | Purpose |\n179: |---|---|\n180: | [`system-skill-advisor/SKILL.md`](../../../SKILL.md) | Runtime instructions for the owning skill. |\n181: | [`sk-code/SKILL.md`](../../../../sk-code/SKILL.md) | OpenCode coding standards and verification routing. |\n182: | [`sk-doc skill-readme-template.md`](../../../../sk-doc/sk-create-skill/assets/skill/skill-readme-template.md) | README structure used for this code README. |"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/README.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794560839199,"end":1794560840177}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794560840187,"sessionID":"ses_e2bd74f77ffeQKrT5GdKIrxQqN","part":{"partID":"prt_1d4294fec001rDQkoKdcZzOAPj","sessionID":"ses_e2bd74f77ffeQKrT5GdKIrxQqN","messageID":"msg_1d428cd19001bqlPGolciGc5ZA","type":"tool","id":"call_9467b634554c47abbcaf34d5","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"ls -1 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research"},"output":"ambiguity-prompts.jsonl\nbuild-holdout.mjs\ncapture-command-routing-baseline.mjs\ncapture-local-native-divergence-ledger.mjs\ncapture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs\ncommand-offer.mjs\ncommand-prompts.jsonl\ncommand-routing-baseline.json\nderive-ambiguity-slice.mjs\ndoctor-prompts.jsonl\ngate3-corpus-runner.mjs\nholdout-prompts.jsonl\nlabeled-prompts.jsonl\nprepare-behaviour-arena.mjs\nREADME.md\nreject-unknown-options.mjs\nrun-command-behaviour.mjs\nscore-command-transcripts.mjs\nscore-outcome-rerank.mjs\nscore-routing-corpus.py\nscorer-eval-baseline.json\nworktree-restore.mjs\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"ambiguity-prompts.jsonl\nbuild-holdout.mjs\ncapture-command-routing-baseline.mjs\ncapture-local-native-divergence-ledger.mjs\ncapture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs\ncommand-offer.mjs\ncommand-prompts.jsonl\ncommand-routing-baseline.json\nderive-ambiguity-slice.mjs\ndoctor-prompts.jsonl\ngate3-corpus-runner.mjs\nholdout-prompts.jsonl\nlabeled-prompts.jsonl\nprepare-behaviour-arena.mjs\nREADME.md\nreject-unknown-options.mjs\nrun-command-behaviour.mjs\nscore-command-transcripts.mjs\nscore-outcome-rerank.mjs\nscore-routing-corpus.py\nscorer-eval-baseline.json\nworktree-restore.mjs\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"ls -1 .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research\"}"},"time":{"start":1794560839660,"end":1794560840187}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794560841396,"sessionID":"ses_e2bd74f77ffeQKrT5GdKIrxQqN","part":{"id":"prt_1d42956b4001sZ9icKeH3OBINf","sessionID":"ses_e2bd74f77ffeQKrT5GdKIrxQqN","messageID":"msg_1d428cd19001bqlPGolciGc5ZA","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"2d743a86ed46819abae9e0c79ac691166d6d675c","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":20738,"output":165,"reasoning":465,"cache":{"read":12288,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794560844614,"sessionID":"ses_e2bd74f77ffeQKrT5GdKIrxQqN","part":{"id":"prt_1d4296346001AZ95JNwUi2vcWF","sessionID":"ses_e2bd74f77ffeQKrT5GdKIrxQqN","messageID":"msg_1d42956b80019doIrTpEw0gVvv","type":"step-start","snapshot":"01fd21d932b3e3f74cf766ba184b3a80c9b06fb4"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794560852645,"sessionID":"ses_e2bd74f77ffeQKrT5GdKIrxQqN","part":{"partID":"prt_1d4297d94001VCov0AVIDS19J6","sessionID":"ses_e2bd74f77ffeQKrT5GdKIrxQqN","messageID":"msg_1d42956b80019doIrTpEw0gVvv","type":"tool","id":"call_4ec5716de99040899f6e9b11","tool":"skill","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"id":"sk-doc"},"output":"<skill_content name=\"sk-doc\">\n# Skill: sk-doc\n\n<!-- Keywords: sk-doc, documentation, markdown, authoring, parent-hub, mode-registry, hub-router, workflowmode, packetkind, sk-create-skill, sk-create-readme, sk-create-agent, sk-create-command, sk-create-feature-catalog, sk-create-manual-testing-playbook, sk-create-benchmark, sk-create-changelog, sk-create-diff, sk-create-repo-rule, sk-create-frontmatter, sk-create-goal, sk-create-with-human-voice, sk-create-quality-control, human-voice-rules, hvr, shared-backbone, create-quality-control-pipeline -->\n\n# Documentation Authoring Hub (sk-doc)\n\nOne advisor identity, fifteen workflow modes across fourteen packets, one shared sk-create-quality-control backbone.\n`sk-doc` is the parent hub for documentation and OpenCode-component authoring.\nIt holds NO per-packet logic: it routes by `workflowMode` through `mode-registry.json`, and each packet keeps its own contract in its nested folder.\nThe cross-cutting sk-create-quality-control pipeline (validators, global standards, frontmatter/llms/template assets) lives once in `shared/` and is consumed by every packet.\n\n**Version authority.** This file's `version` frontmatter is the hub's release version and matches the newest entry under `changelog/`; `description.json`, `mode-registry.json` and `hub-router.json` carry the same value, so every hub-root JSON routing file states the same release; `ROUTER.md` keeps its own document version.\n\n---\n\n## 1. WHEN TO USE\n\nUse this skill for documentation and OpenCode-component authoring, and for document-quality work. The hub classifies the request, resolves a `workflowMode`, and loads the matching nested packet.\n\n| Mode | Use it for | Packet | Command |\n|------|------------|--------|---------|\n| **sk-create-skill** | Scaffold an OpenCode skill (and, via `sk-create-skill-parent`, a parent hub with nested mode packets) | `sk-create-skill/` | `/create:skill`, `/create:skill-parent` |\n| **sk-create-readme** | Author a folder README, general or code-folder | `sk-create-readme/` | `/create:readme` |\n| **sk-create-agent** | Scaffold an OpenCode agent (permission/authority frontmatter) | `sk-create-agent/` | `/create:agent` |\n| **sk-create-command** | Scaffold an OpenCode slash command (argument-hint + allowed-tools + router/presentation split) | `sk-create-command/` | `/create:command` |\n| **sk-create-feature-catalog** | Author a feature-catalog inventory package | `sk-create-feature-catalog/` | `/create:feature-catalog` |\n| **sk-create-manual-testing-playbook** | Author a manual-testing-playbook package | `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/` | `/create:manual-testing-playbook` |\n| **sk-create-benchmark** | Author MCP-promotion and behavior packages or inputs | `sk-create-benchmark/` | `/create:benchmark` |\n| **sk-create-changelog** | Author a global component changelog entry (version bump + topology-aware placement). Packet-local changelogs are deprecated | `sk-create-changelog/` | `/create:changelog` |\n| **sk-create-diff** | Produce a local, Git-free before/after review of an edited document (text/Markdown/HTML/DOCX/text-PDF) as a self-contained HTML report | `sk-create-diff/` | `/create:diff` |\n| **sk-create-repo-rule** | Create, revise or retire a repo-local rule under `repo-rules/` and wire it into `REPO RULES.md` (four decision tests refuse most requests) | `sk-create-repo-rule/` | `/create:repo-rule` |\n| **sk-create-with-human-voice** | Apply the Human Voice Rules to prose, or score prose against them, with a scope gate and a re-scan | `sk-create-with-human-voice/` | `/create:with-human-voice` |\n| **sk-create-frontmatter** | Own the YAML frontmatter contract: per-class field rules, the description budget, and the 4-part `version` derivation | `sk-create-frontmatter/` | — (routes via aliases) |\n| **sk-create-goal** | Author or revise a packet `goal.md`: the durable directive, three to seven self-contained completion criteria, and the phase-parent binding table (session-goal setting stays with the goal hooks) | `sk-create-goal/` | `/create:goal` |\n| **sk-create-quality-control** | Validate / score / optimize an EXISTING document (extract → DQI → HVR → validate) | `sk-create-quality-control/` | (routes via aliases) |\n\n### When NOT to Use\n- Code implementation, tests, or debugging — use `sk-code`.\n- Git worktree, branch, commit, PR, or release work — use `sk-git`.\n- Spec-folder workflow / memory / save context — use `system-spec-kit`.\n- A measured Style Reference (design tokens extracted from a live source) — use `sk-design-md-generator`.\n- The `shared/` backbone is consumed by the packets, not invoked as a user workflow.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SMART ROUTING\n\nRouting is **registry-driven at runtime and authored in `hub-router.json`** in two stages.\nStage 1 (hub → mode) selects a mode: `hub-router.json` `vocabularyClasses` is the one authored routing vocabulary.\nThe `mode-registry.json` aliases, each packet's `Keyword triggers:` line and the root `ROUTER.md` intents are checked against it.\n`sk-create-skill/scripts/ci-hub-vocabulary-source.cjs` fails on the `alias`, `stage-two`, `triggers` and `packets` legs; its `stage-two-reverse` leg only reports.\nStage 2 (mode → leaves) lives in the root [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md), which maps the request's authoring intent to the packet-local leaf resources that mode loads.\nThe two layers stay separate: the hub never emits leaf paths, and the surface router never re-decides the mode.\nThe advisor routes every documentation and authoring query to the single identity `sk-doc`.\nResolve the mode with `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-doc --prompt \"<task>\"`.\nAct on the decision: `route` loads its `targets`, `clarify` and `defer` ask, `reject` refuses.\nCompiled routing is the default.\nAn unclear documentation intent asks for disambiguation, because `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`.\nRun the compiled-routing directive in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting hub resources.\n\n---\n\n## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n\n### Terms\n\n- **`workflowMode`**: the public packet key a request resolves to, such as `sk-create-skill`.\n- **`packetKind`**: `workflow` for every sk-doc packet, because this hub has no surface axis.\n- **`backendKind`**: `template-scaffold` for the create-* generators and `sk-create-quality-control` for the quality-control mode.\n- **packet**: a nested `sk-create-*` folder that keeps its own authoring contract, references, assets and scripts.\n- **`mode-registry.json`**: the registry that owns each packet's discriminator, aliases, command and `advisorRouting`.\n- **`hub-router.json`**: stage one's signals, vocabulary classes, default fallback resource and bundle rules.\n- **`vocabularyClasses`**: the one authored routing vocabulary that the registry aliases, the `Keyword triggers:` lines and the `ROUTER.md` intents are checked against.\n- **`ROUTER.md`**: stage two's `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP`, which map an authoring intent to the packet-local leaf resources its mode loads.\n- **leaf**: a canonical `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair from `leaf-manifest.json` that a `RESOURCE_MAP` path converts to.\n- **`defer`**: the outcome that asks for disambiguation, which this hub reaches because `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`.\n- **shared backbone**: the `shared/` copy of the sk-create-quality-control pipeline that every packet consumes.\n- **`graph-metadata.json`**: the one advisor identity for the whole skill, which no packet or `shared/` directory carries.\n\n### Layout\n```\nsk-doc/\n  SKILL.md               # this routing hub (no per-packet logic)\n  ROUTER.md              # stage-two surface router (authoring intent -> leaf sets)\n  mode-registry.json     # the fifteen-mode discriminator + advisorRouting (single source of truth)\n  hub-router.json        # router signals + vocabulary classes\n  description.json       # hub advisor descriptor\n  graph-metadata.json    # the ONE advisor identity for the whole skill\n  changelog/  manual-testing-playbook/  benchmark/  feature-catalog/\n  sk-create-skill/  sk-create-readme/  sk-create-agent/  sk-create-command/\n  sk-create-feature-catalog/  sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/\n  sk-create-benchmark/  sk-create-changelog/  sk-create-diff/\n  sk-create-repo-rule/  sk-create-frontmatter/  sk-create-goal/\n  sk-create-with-human-voice/  sk-create-quality-control/    # nested workflow packets\n  scripts/               # facade symlinks -> shared/ + owning packets (tool paths only)\n  shared/                # sk-create-quality-control backbone: validators, global standards, shared assets\n```\n\nEach packet is self-contained (its own `SKILL.md`, `README.md`, `changelog/`, and moved `references/`/`assets/`/`scripts/`) and carries **no** `graph-metadata.json`, so the advisor discovers exactly one `sk-doc` identity.\n\n### Shared backbone\n`shared/` holds the universal sk-create-quality-control pipeline consumed by every packet:\n- Generic validator scripts in `shared/scripts/`.\n- Cross-cutting standards and vocabulary in `shared/references/`.\n- Shared templates in `shared/assets/`.\n\nThe generic validator `shared/scripts/cite-drift-scan.mjs` counts and resolves the `<path>.<ext>:<line>` citations in every tracked skill doc.\nIt makes zero model calls by default.\n`--jev` runs the backend and needs `--out <dir>`, so every call is recorded, and the Jev arm and the advisory live in the sibling `shared/scripts/classifier-cite-drift-scan.mjs`.\n`validate_document.py` runs the same check as a non-blocking advisory on the document it validates.\nIt prints `cite-drift advisory:` lines only when a Jev credential is stored, never changes the exit code, and `JEV_FEATURE_CITE_DRIFT=0` (older name: `SKDOC_CITE_DRIFT_CHECK=0`) skips it.\nThe `sk-doc/scripts/` root directory keeps facade symlinks pointing inward to `shared/` and the owning packets so tool paths resolve.\nThere are no hub-root `assets/` or `references/` aggregation directories: consumers reference each packet's own `assets/`/`references/` or the `shared/` backbone directly.\n\n---\n\n## 4. RULES\n\n### ✅ ALWAYS\n- **ALWAYS** resolve a packet through `mode-registry.json`; never hardcode a router mapping in the hub.\n- **ALWAYS** keep authoring contracts in the packets; the hub stays routing-only.\n- **ALWAYS** keep exactly one `graph-metadata.json` (this hub's) so the advisor sees one identity.\n- **ALWAYS** keep the root `ROUTER.md` `RESOURCE_MAP` in sync with `leaf-manifest.json` and `leaf-aliases.json` — the leaf sets dual-read to canonical typed pairs at the one contract boundary.\n- **ALWAYS** keep the sk-create-quality-control pipeline as one shared source under `shared/`, consumed by the packets.\n- **ALWAYS** keep changelogs as real files at the hub and in each packet — never symlinked.\n\n### ⛔ NEVER\n- **NEVER** add a `graph-metadata.json` inside a packet or `shared/`.\n- **NEVER** put per-packet authoring logic in the hub.\n- **NEVER** add a surface axis or a `surfaceBundle` outcome — sk-doc is workflow-only.\n\n### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n- A new documentation workflow is needed — extend `mode-registry.json` and open a packet; do not bolt logic onto the hub.\n- A packet would become a near-empty shell (generic doctrine, no type-specific behavior) — fold it into `shared/` instead.\n\n---\n\n## 5. REFERENCES\n\n- Registry: `mode-registry.json` (fifteen modes across fourteen packets, `packetKind: workflow`).\n- Hub router: `hub-router.json` (signals + vocabulary classes).\n- Surface router: `ROUTER.md` (authoring intent to packet-local leaf sets).\n- Advisor descriptor: `description.json`; skill-graph identity: `graph-metadata.json`.\n- Packets: `sk-create-skill/`, `sk-create-readme/`, `sk-create-agent/`, `sk-create-command/`, `sk-create-feature-catalog/`, `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/`, `sk-create-benchmark/`, `sk-create-changelog/`, `sk-create-diff/`, `sk-create-repo-rule/`, `sk-create-frontmatter/`, `sk-create-goal/`, `sk-create-with-human-voice/`, `sk-create-quality-control/`.\n- Shared backbone: `shared/scripts/`, `shared/references/`, `shared/assets/`.\n- Parent-skill pattern: `sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md`.\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc\nRelative paths in this skill (e.g., scripts/, reference/) are relative to this base directory.\nNote: file list is sampled.\n\n<skill_files>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/ROUTER.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/failed-runs.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/results.csv</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/source.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal/results.csv</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal/source.md</file>\n</skill_files>\n</skill_content>","title":"skill","metadata":{"metadata":{"name":"sk-doc","directory":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc","truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"<skill_content name=\"sk-doc\">\n# Skill: sk-doc\n\n<!-- Keywords: sk-doc, documentation, markdown, authoring, parent-hub, mode-registry, hub-router, workflowmode, packetkind, sk-create-skill, sk-create-readme, sk-create-agent, sk-create-command, sk-create-feature-catalog, sk-create-manual-testing-playbook, sk-create-benchmark, sk-create-changelog, sk-create-diff, sk-create-repo-rule, sk-create-frontmatter, sk-create-goal, sk-create-with-human-voice, sk-create-quality-control, human-voice-rules, hvr, shared-backbone, create-quality-control-pipeline -->\n\n# Documentation Authoring Hub (sk-doc)\n\nOne advisor identity, fifteen workflow modes across fourteen packets, one shared sk-create-quality-control backbone.\n`sk-doc` is the parent hub for documentation and OpenCode-component authoring.\nIt holds NO per-packet logic: it routes by `workflowMode` through `mode-registry.json`, and each packet keeps its own contract in its nested folder.\nThe cross-cutting sk-create-quality-control pipeline (validators, global standards, frontmatter/llms/template assets) lives once in `shared/` and is consumed by every packet.\n\n**Version authority.** This file's `version` frontmatter is the hub's release version and matches the newest entry under `changelog/`; `description.json`, `mode-registry.json` and `hub-router.json` carry the same value, so every hub-root JSON routing file states the same release; `ROUTER.md` keeps its own document version.\n\n---\n\n## 1. WHEN TO USE\n\nUse this skill for documentation and OpenCode-component authoring, and for document-quality work. The hub classifies the request, resolves a `workflowMode`, and loads the matching nested packet.\n\n| Mode | Use it for | Packet | Command |\n|------|------------|--------|---------|\n| **sk-create-skill** | Scaffold an OpenCode skill (and, via `sk-create-skill-parent`, a parent hub with nested mode packets) | `sk-create-skill/` | `/create:skill`, `/create:skill-parent` |\n| **sk-create-readme** | Author a folder README, general or code-folder | `sk-create-readme/` | `/create:readme` |\n| **sk-create-agent** | Scaffold an OpenCode agent (permission/authority frontmatter) | `sk-create-agent/` | `/create:agent` |\n| **sk-create-command** | Scaffold an OpenCode slash command (argument-hint + allowed-tools + router/presentation split) | `sk-create-command/` | `/create:command` |\n| **sk-create-feature-catalog** | Author a feature-catalog inventory package | `sk-create-feature-catalog/` | `/create:feature-catalog` |\n| **sk-create-manual-testing-playbook** | Author a manual-testing-playbook package | `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/` | `/create:manual-testing-playbook` |\n| **sk-create-benchmark** | Author MCP-promotion and behavior packages or inputs | `sk-create-benchmark/` | `/create:benchmark` |\n| **sk-create-changelog** | Author a global component changelog entry (version bump + topology-aware placement). Packet-local changelogs are deprecated | `sk-create-changelog/` | `/create:changelog` |\n| **sk-create-diff** | Produce a local, Git-free before/after review of an edited document (text/Markdown/HTML/DOCX/text-PDF) as a self-contained HTML report | `sk-create-diff/` | `/create:diff` |\n| **sk-create-repo-rule** | Create, revise or retire a repo-local rule under `repo-rules/` and wire it into `REPO RULES.md` (four decision tests refuse most requests) | `sk-create-repo-rule/` | `/create:repo-rule` |\n| **sk-create-with-human-voice** | Apply the Human Voice Rules to prose, or score prose against them, with a scope gate and a re-scan | `sk-create-with-human-voice/` | `/create:with-human-voice` |\n| **sk-create-frontmatter** | Own the YAML frontmatter contract: per-class field rules, the description budget, and the 4-part `version` derivation | `sk-create-frontmatter/` | — (routes via aliases) |\n| **sk-create-goal** | Author or revise a packet `goal.md`: the durable directive, three to seven self-contained completion criteria, and the phase-parent binding table (session-goal setting stays with the goal hooks) | `sk-create-goal/` | `/create:goal` |\n| **sk-create-quality-control** | Validate / score / optimize an EXISTING document (extract → DQI → HVR → validate) | `sk-create-quality-control/` | (routes via aliases) |\n\n### When NOT to Use\n- Code implementation, tests, or debugging — use `sk-code`.\n- Git worktree, branch, commit, PR, or release work — use `sk-git`.\n- Spec-folder workflow / memory / save context — use `system-spec-kit`.\n- A measured Style Reference (design tokens extracted from a live source) — use `sk-design-md-generator`.\n- The `shared/` backbone is consumed by the packets, not invoked as a user workflow.\n\n---\n\n## 2. SMART ROUTING\n\nRouting is **registry-driven at runtime and authored in `hub-router.json`** in two stages.\nStage 1 (hub → mode) selects a mode: `hub-router.json` `vocabularyClasses` is the one authored routing vocabulary.\nThe `mode-registry.json` aliases, each packet's `Keyword triggers:` line and the root `ROUTER.md` intents are checked against it.\n`sk-create-skill/scripts/ci-hub-vocabulary-source.cjs` fails on the `alias`, `stage-two`, `triggers` and `packets` legs; its `stage-two-reverse` leg only reports.\nStage 2 (mode → leaves) lives in the root [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md), which maps the request's authoring intent to the packet-local leaf resources that mode loads.\nThe two layers stay separate: the hub never emits leaf paths, and the surface router never re-decides the mode.\nThe advisor routes every documentation and authoring query to the single identity `sk-doc`.\nResolve the mode with `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-doc --prompt \"<task>\"`.\nAct on the decision: `route` loads its `targets`, `clarify` and `defer` ask, `reject` refuses.\nCompiled routing is the default.\nAn unclear documentation intent asks for disambiguation, because `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`.\nRun the compiled-routing directive in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting hub resources.\n\n---\n\n## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n\n### Terms\n\n- **`workflowMode`**: the public packet key a request resolves to, such as `sk-create-skill`.\n- **`packetKind`**: `workflow` for every sk-doc packet, because this hub has no surface axis.\n- **`backendKind`**: `template-scaffold` for the create-* generators and `sk-create-quality-control` for the quality-control mode.\n- **packet**: a nested `sk-create-*` folder that keeps its own authoring contract, references, assets and scripts.\n- **`mode-registry.json`**: the registry that owns each packet's discriminator, aliases, command and `advisorRouting`.\n- **`hub-router.json`**: stage one's signals, vocabulary classes, default fallback resource and bundle rules.\n- **`vocabularyClasses`**: the one authored routing vocabulary that the registry aliases, the `Keyword triggers:` lines and the `ROUTER.md` intents are checked against.\n- **`ROUTER.md`**: stage two's `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP`, which map an authoring intent to the packet-local leaf resources its mode loads.\n- **leaf**: a canonical `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair from `leaf-manifest.json` that a `RESOURCE_MAP` path converts to.\n- **`defer`**: the outcome that asks for disambiguation, which this hub reaches because `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`.\n- **shared backbone**: the `shared/` copy of the sk-create-quality-control pipeline that every packet consumes.\n- **`graph-metadata.json`**: the one advisor identity for the whole skill, which no packet or `shared/` directory carries.\n\n### Layout\n```\nsk-doc/\n  SKILL.md               # this routing hub (no per-packet logic)\n  ROUTER.md              # stage-two surface router (authoring intent -> leaf sets)\n  mode-registry.json     # the fifteen-mode discriminator + advisorRouting (single source of truth)\n  hub-router.json        # router signals + vocabulary classes\n  description.json       # hub advisor descriptor\n  graph-metadata.json    # the ONE advisor identity for the whole skill\n  changelog/  manual-testing-playbook/  benchmark/  feature-catalog/\n  sk-create-skill/  sk-create-readme/  sk-create-agent/  sk-create-command/\n  sk-create-feature-catalog/  sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/\n  sk-create-benchmark/  sk-create-changelog/  sk-create-diff/\n  sk-create-repo-rule/  sk-create-frontmatter/  sk-create-goal/\n  sk-create-with-human-voice/  sk-create-quality-control/    # nested workflow packets\n  scripts/               # facade symlinks -> shared/ + owning packets (tool paths only)\n  shared/                # sk-create-quality-control backbone: validators, global standards, shared assets\n```\n\nEach packet is self-contained (its own `SKILL.md`, `README.md`, `changelog/`, and moved `references/`/`assets/`/`scripts/`) and carries **no** `graph-metadata.json`, so the advisor discovers exactly one `sk-doc` identity.\n\n### Shared backbone\n`shared/` holds the universal sk-create-quality-control pipeline consumed by every packet:\n- Generic validator scripts in `shared/scripts/`.\n- Cross-cutting standards and vocabulary in `shared/references/`.\n- Shared templates in `shared/assets/`.\n\nThe generic validator `shared/scripts/cite-drift-scan.mjs` counts and resolves the `<path>.<ext>:<line>` citations in every tracked skill doc.\nIt makes zero model calls by default.\n`--jev` runs the backend and needs `--out <dir>`, so every call is recorded, and the Jev arm and the advisory live in the sibling `shared/scripts/classifier-cite-drift-scan.mjs`.\n`validate_document.py` runs the same check as a non-blocking advisory on the document it validates.\nIt prints `cite-drift advisory:` lines only when a Jev credential is stored, never changes the exit code, and `JEV_FEATURE_CITE_DRIFT=0` (older name: `SKDOC_CITE_DRIFT_CHECK=0`) skips it.\nThe `sk-doc/scripts/` root directory keeps facade symlinks pointing inward to `shared/` and the owning packets so tool paths resolve.\nThere are no hub-root `assets/` or `references/` aggregation directories: consumers reference each packet's own `assets/`/`references/` or the `shared/` backbone directly.\n\n---\n\n## 4. RULES\n\n### ✅ ALWAYS\n- **ALWAYS** resolve a packet through `mode-registry.json`; never hardcode a router mapping in the hub.\n- **ALWAYS** keep authoring contracts in the packets; the hub stays routing-only.\n- **ALWAYS** keep exactly one `graph-metadata.json` (this hub's) so the advisor sees one identity.\n- **ALWAYS** keep the root `ROUTER.md` `RESOURCE_MAP` in sync with `leaf-manifest.json` and `leaf-aliases.json` — the leaf sets dual-read to canonical typed pairs at the one contract boundary.\n- **ALWAYS** keep the sk-create-quality-control pipeline as one shared source under `shared/`, consumed by the packets.\n- **ALWAYS** keep changelogs as real files at the hub and in each packet — never symlinked.\n\n### ⛔ NEVER\n- **NEVER** add a `graph-metadata.json` inside a packet or `shared/`.\n- **NEVER** put per-packet authoring logic in the hub.\n- **NEVER** add a surface axis or a `surfaceBundle` outcome — sk-doc is workflow-only.\n\n### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n- A new documentation workflow is needed — extend `mode-registry.json` and open a packet; do not bolt logic onto the hub.\n- A packet would become a near-empty shell (generic doctrine, no type-specific behavior) — fold it into `shared/` instead.\n\n---\n\n## 5. REFERENCES\n\n- Registry: `mode-registry.json` (fifteen modes across fourteen packets, `packetKind: workflow`).\n- Hub router: `hub-router.json` (signals + vocabulary classes).\n- Surface router: `ROUTER.md` (authoring intent to packet-local leaf sets).\n- Advisor descriptor: `description.json`; skill-graph identity: `graph-metadata.json`.\n- Packets: `sk-create-skill/`, `sk-create-readme/`, `sk-create-agent/`, `sk-create-command/`, `sk-create-feature-catalog/`, `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/`, `sk-create-benchmark/`, `sk-create-changelog/`, `sk-create-diff/`, `sk-create-repo-rule/`, `sk-create-frontmatter/`, `sk-create-goal/`, `sk-create-with-human-voice/`, `sk-create-quality-control/`.\n- Shared backbone: `shared/scripts/`, `shared/references/`, `shared/assets/`.\n- Parent-skill pattern: `sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md`.\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc\nRelative paths in this skill (e.g., scripts/, reference/) are relative to this base directory.\nNote: file list is sampled.\n\n<skill_files>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/ROUTER.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/failed-runs.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/results.csv</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal--review-remediation/source.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal/README.md</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal/results.csv</file>\n<file>/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-doc/benchmark/reports/2026-09-26--manual-testing-playbook--create-goal/source.md</file>\n</skill_files>\n</skill_content>"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"id\":\"sk-doc\"}"},"time":{"start":1794560851348,"end":1794560852645}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794560852652,"sessionID":"ses_e2bd74f77ffeQKrT5GdKIrxQqN","part":{"partID":"prt_1d4297e31001tr9KEYBVwSbp9J","sessionID":"ses_e2bd74f77ffeQKrT5GdKIrxQqN","messageID":"msg_1d42956b80019doIrTpEw0gVvv","type":"tool","id":"call_87b54ec3d2c84e8aafee4a4c","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"for f in build-holdout.mjs capture-local-native-divergence-ledger.mjs capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs derive-ambiguity-slice.mjs prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs reject-unknown-options.mjs score-outcome-rerank.mjs worktree-restore.mjs; do echo \"===== $f\"; sed -n '1,40p' \".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/$f\"; done","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research"},"output":"===== build-holdout.mjs\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: Independent Holdout Builder\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n//\n// Assembles a frozen, independent holdout from three separately-authored,\n// real-labeled fixtures that are all disjoint from the training corpus the\n// explicit-lane rules were tuned against. No gold is invented: every row's\n// label is copied from its source fixture's own expected field.\n//\n//   - harder-intent corpus   (expectedSkill)     — deliberately hard prompts\n//   - regression cases        (expected_top_any)  — P0/P1 regression prompts\n//   - executor-delegation     (expectedTop)       — executor routing branches\n//\n// Provenance is preserved per row via `source_type` + `origin_fixture` so the\n// holdout is auditable and never mistaken for freshly-harvested production\n// traffic. Harvesting and hand-labeling real advisor-hook telemetry is the\n// legitimate future upgrade and is deliberately NOT faked here.\n//\n// Dedup is family-normalized (lowercase + punctuation strip + whitespace\n// collapse): first occurrence within the pool wins, and any row colliding with\n// a training-corpus prompt is dropped so the holdout stays independent.\n//\n// Usage:\n//   node build-holdout.mjs            # print summary only\n//   node build-holdout.mjs --write    # (re)write holdout-prompts.jsonl\n\nimport { createHash } from 'node:crypto';\nimport { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\nimport { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\nimport { rejectUnknownOptions } from './reject-unknown-options.mjs';\n\nconst HERE = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));\nconst HARDER_TS = resolve(HERE, '../../tests/scorer/fixtures/harder-intent-prompt-corpus.ts');\nconst REGRESSION_JSONL = resolve(HERE, '../fixtures/skill-advisor-regression-cases.jsonl');\nconst DELEGATION_JSON = resolve(HERE, '../../tests/parity/fixtures/executor-delegation-cases.json');\nconst CORPUS_JSONL = resolve(HERE, 'labeled-prompts.jsonl');\nconst OUTPUT_JSONL = resolve(HERE, 'holdout-prompts.jsonl');\n\nfunction normalizeFamily(prompt) {\n===== capture-local-native-divergence-ledger.mjs\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: Local-vs-native divergence ledger capture\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n//\n// Recomputes the strict Python/native top-1 divergence set used by\n// local-native-divergence-ratchet.vitest.ts. Existing approvals are retained\n// only when their prompt hash and both current tops still match. The output is\n// therefore a capture of live source behavior, not a hand-edited fixture.\n//\n// It scores in the regime the ratchet replays: both scorers read a skill graph\n// indexed fresh from this checkout into a temporary directory, and the clock is\n// pinned to midnight UTC of the capture day, which the ledger records as its\n// scoringInstant. Requires a built dist (npm run build).\n//\n// Usage:\n//   node capture-local-native-divergence-ledger.mjs\n//   node capture-local-native-divergence-ledger.mjs --write\n//   node capture-local-native-divergence-ledger.mjs --write --summary\n\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 1. IMPORTS\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nimport { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\nimport { createHash } from 'node:crypto';\nimport { existsSync, mkdtempSync, readFileSync, rmSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { tmpdir } from 'node:os';\nimport { basename, dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\nimport { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\n\n// This script must run as a lone copy (its parity test copies only this file),\n// so it keeps its own option check instead of importing the shared helper.\nfunction rejectUnknownOptions() {\n  const scriptName = basename(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));\n  const known = new Set(['--write', '--summary']);\n  for (const token of process.argv.slice(2)) {\n    if (known.has(token)) continue;\n    process.stderr.write(`${scriptName}: unknown option: ${token}\\n`);\n    process.stderr.write(`usage: ${scriptName} [--write] [--summary]\\n`);\n    process.exit(2);\n===== capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: SCORER EVAL BASELINE CAPTURE\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n//\n// Captures the current scorer accuracy across the full corpus, the independent\n// holdout, the frozen ambiguity slice, and the named intent buckets, under the\n// reproducible filesystem projection (empty SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DB_DIR → SQLite\n// loader returns null → projection built purely from committed graph metadata).\n// The result is the ratchet baseline: the ratchet gate re-scores live under the\n// same env and holds every metric to it, so any scoring or metadata change that\n// moves a number forces a conscious re-capture rather than drifting silently.\n//\n// The *Sha256 fields pin the baseline to the exact fixtures it was captured\n// against; a fixture edit changes a hash and forces re-baselining.\n//\n// Usage:\n//   node capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs            # print JSON to stdout\n//   node capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs --write     # also write the baseline\n//   node capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs --misses    # print each failing prompt, one JSON line each\n//   SPECKIT_EVAL_BASELINE_SCORING_DATE=YYYY-MM-DD node capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs --write\n//                                                     # move the pinned scoring date\n\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 1. IMPORTS\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nimport { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\nimport { createHash } from 'node:crypto';\nimport { mkdtempSync, readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { tmpdir } from 'node:os';\nimport { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\nimport { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\nimport { rejectUnknownOptions } from './reject-unknown-options.mjs';\n\nrejectUnknownOptions(import.meta.url, ['--write', '--misses']);\n\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 2. CONSTANTS\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n===== derive-ambiguity-slice.mjs\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: Empirical Ambiguity Slice Deriver\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n//\n// The labeled corpus carries no expected-ambiguous labels, so an ambiguity\n// false-positive/negative rate cannot be computed. What CAN be measured\n// honestly is top-1 accuracy on the hardest (lowest top-2 margin) prompts: a\n// regression that only hurts contested prompts is invisible in the aggregate\n// but fails this slice.\n//\n// The slice is derived once and frozen: the top-2 fused-score margin is\n// computed for every corpus row under the reproducible filesystem projection,\n// and rows with `margin <= tau` are kept with their existing gold label. tau is\n// chosen as the tightest genuinely-contested band (a meaningful minority, well\n// inside the near-tie window), and both tau and the resulting row-id set are\n// frozen so the slice never silently re-derives on a corpus edit.\n//\n// The frozen `margin_at_capture` is advisory (it documents why a row is in the\n// slice); the gate recomputes margin live and does not trust it.\n//\n// Determinism: an empty SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DB_DIR forces the SQLite loader to\n// return null, yielding the clean filesystem projection built purely from\n// committed graph metadata — reproducible from the repo alone.\n//\n// Usage:\n//   node derive-ambiguity-slice.mjs            # print summary only\n//   node derive-ambiguity-slice.mjs --write    # (re)write ambiguity-prompts.jsonl\n\nimport { mkdtempSync, readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { tmpdir } from 'node:os';\nimport { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\nimport { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\nimport { rejectUnknownOptions } from './reject-unknown-options.mjs';\n\nrejectUnknownOptions(import.meta.url, ['--write']);\n\n// The tightest contested margin band: a meaningful minority of the corpus.\nconst TAU = 0.03;\nconst CAPTURED_AT = '2026-07-07';\n\n===== prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: Behaviour Arena Builder\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n//\n// A live turn has to run where it cannot reach anything outside its own\n// checkout, and where the advisor it measures is built the way a user gets it.\n// A clone of the branch tip in a directory git does not own gives both. The\n// clone has no remote to push to and no outer repository whose hooks or\n// credentials it could borrow, and its tracked tree is exactly the commit under\n// test, so a per-turn restore has a clean baseline to put back.\n//\n// A clone also leaves out every ignored build output. The advisor under test\n// runs from compiled JavaScript and its SQLite skill graph, so those trees are\n// copied in afterwards. Building them inside the clone instead would measure a\n// bundle nobody reviewed, and would need the network while an arena is meant to\n// be offline.\n//\n// A copy also has to look like a build. The freshness guard each CLI shim runs\n// falls back to comparing the newest watched source against the build's own\n// timestamp, and a checkout stamps every source with the clone time while the\n// copied build keeps the moment it was compiled. Left alone, that order says\n// every copied package is stale and every shim exits before running. The copied\n// build is the same commit's build, so its timestamp is set to the copy time,\n// which is what a build in this checkout would carry.\n//\n// Nothing is copied before the destination is checked. A destination inside a\n// repository would inherit that repository's hooks and ignore rules, and a\n// destination that already exists may hold another arm's log, so both are\n// refused rather than merged into.\n//\n// The copy reads the source worktree, so the one thing it must never carry\n// across is a dotenv file. A model reads and writes an arena, so a secret that\n// followed the build output in would sit where a run can print it. Every file\n// whose name begins with `.env` is skipped while copying, at every depth.\n//\n// Usage:\n//   node prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs --source <repo root> --dest <dir>\n\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 1. IMPORTS\n===== reject-unknown-options.mjs\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: Shared unknown-option guard\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n//\n// A misspelled flag must fail before any work runs: silently ignoring it\n// would run the default behavior and make the no-op look successful.\n\nimport { basename } from 'node:path';\nimport { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\n\n/**\n * Exit 2 on the first argument outside a script's own option list.\n *\n * @param {string} scriptUrl - The calling module's import.meta.url.\n * @param {string[]} knownOptions - Every argument the caller accepts.\n */\nexport function rejectUnknownOptions(scriptUrl, knownOptions) {\n  const scriptName = basename(fileURLToPath(scriptUrl));\n  const known = new Set(knownOptions);\n  for (const token of process.argv.slice(2)) {\n    if (known.has(token)) continue;\n    process.stderr.write(`${scriptName}: unknown option: ${token}\\n`);\n    process.stderr.write(`usage: ${scriptName} ${knownOptions.map((option) => `[${option}]`).join(' ')}\\n`);\n    process.exit(2);\n  }\n}\n===== score-outcome-rerank.mjs\n#!/usr/bin/env node\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// COMPONENT: OUTCOME-WEIGHTED RERANK ROUTING-ACCURACY EVAL DRIVER\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n//\n// Measures whether the outcome-weighted re-rank improves routing accuracy over\n// the similarity-only baseline, reported as MRR (mean reciprocal rank of the\n// gold skill) AND right-skill@3 — not @1 alone. A near-tie reorder mainly moves\n// a skill between adjacent ranks, so @1 is a coarse, noisy lens; MRR and @3 see\n// the fuller ranking shift and are the gate this packet reads.\n//\n// The flag governs `outcomeWeightedRerank` (lib/scorer/outcome-weighted-rerank),\n// which blends live fused similarity with an observed-outcome reliability term\n// through the shared Beta posterior. This eval scores the rerank's full ordering\n// against the similarity-only ordering it reorders.\n//\n// Honest signal, not leakage: the reliability fold is built from a TRAIN split\n// of the labeled corpus (skill marked correct => success, wrong => failure),\n// then accuracy is measured ONLY on a disjoint HELD-OUT split. A flag that\n// merely memorised the labels would not transfer across the split.\n//\n// READ-ONLY: scores against the live skill-graph.sqlite via a read path; never\n// mutates any database.\n\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 1. IMPORTS\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nimport { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\nimport { dirname, join } from 'node:path';\n\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 2. CONSTANTS\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nconst SCRIPT_DIR = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));\n// routing-accuracy -> scripts -> mcp_server\nconst MCP_SERVER = join(SCRIPT_DIR, '..', '..');\nconst REPO_ROOT = join(MCP_SERVER, '..', '..', '..', '..');\nconst CORPUS = join(SCRIPT_DIR, 'labeled-prompts.jsonl');\n\n===== worktree-restore.mjs\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: Worktree restore\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n//\n// A measured turn runs inside a checkout someone else may be working in, so the\n// runner has to put back exactly what the turn changed and nothing else. The\n// snapshot is the baseline: any path that was already dirty when the turn began\n// belongs to an earlier edit and is never touched, even when the turn adds that\n// path to the index on top of the edit.\n//\n// Restoring from the index is not enough. A staged new file is in the index, so\n// restoring from the index keeps it, while only HEAD is the state the turn\n// started from. A path HEAD knows is checked out from HEAD, and a path HEAD does\n// not know is dropped from the index and deleted from the worktree. A second\n// snapshot after the restore reports what is still different, so the caller can\n// fail the run instead of claiming a clean worktree.\n\nimport { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\nimport { rmSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { resolve } from 'node:path';\n\n// A filesystem monitor that decides a path is unchanged makes a status snapshot\n// lie, and the restore trusts that snapshot.\nconst GIT_PREFIX = ['-c', 'core.fsmonitor=false'];\n\nfunction git(cwd, args) {\n  const result = spawnSync('git', [...GIT_PREFIX, ...args], { cwd, encoding: 'utf8' });\n  if (result.error) throw result.error;\n  return result;\n}\n\nfunction trimTrailingSlash(path) {\n  return path.endsWith('/') ? path.slice(0, -1) : path;\n}\n\n/**\n * Read the working tree state of one checkout.\n *\n * Porcelain names each path relative to the checkout, and a rename names both\n * the old and the new path. Both sides are separate keys because both have to\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"===== build-holdout.mjs\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: Independent Holdout Builder\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n//\n// Assembles a frozen, independent holdout from three separately-authored,\n// real-labeled fixtures that are all disjoint from the training corpus the\n// explicit-lane rules were tuned against. No gold is invented: every row's\n// label is copied from its source fixture's own expected field.\n//\n//   - harder-intent corpus   (expectedSkill)     — deliberately hard prompts\n//   - regression cases        (expected_top_any)  — P0/P1 regression prompts\n//   - executor-delegation     (expectedTop)       — executor routing branches\n//\n// Provenance is preserved per row via `source_type` + `origin_fixture` so the\n// holdout is auditable and never mistaken for freshly-harvested production\n// traffic. Harvesting and hand-labeling real advisor-hook telemetry is the\n// legitimate future upgrade and is deliberately NOT faked here.\n//\n// Dedup is family-normalized (lowercase + punctuation strip + whitespace\n// collapse): first occurrence within the pool wins, and any row colliding with\n// a training-corpus prompt is dropped so the holdout stays independent.\n//\n// Usage:\n//   node build-holdout.mjs            # print summary only\n//   node build-holdout.mjs --write    # (re)write holdout-prompts.jsonl\n\nimport { createHash } from 'node:crypto';\nimport { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\nimport { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\nimport { rejectUnknownOptions } from './reject-unknown-options.mjs';\n\nconst HERE = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));\nconst HARDER_TS = resolve(HERE, '../../tests/scorer/fixtures/harder-intent-prompt-corpus.ts');\nconst REGRESSION_JSONL = resolve(HERE, '../fixtures/skill-advisor-regression-cases.jsonl');\nconst DELEGATION_JSON = resolve(HERE, '../../tests/parity/fixtures/executor-delegation-cases.json');\nconst CORPUS_JSONL = resolve(HERE, 'labeled-prompts.jsonl');\nconst OUTPUT_JSONL = resolve(HERE, 'holdout-prompts.jsonl');\n\nfunction normalizeFamily(prompt) {\n===== capture-local-native-divergence-ledger.mjs\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: Local-vs-native divergence ledger capture\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n//\n// Recomputes the strict Python/native top-1 divergence set used by\n// local-native-divergence-ratchet.vitest.ts. Existing approvals are retained\n// only when their prompt hash and both current tops still match. The output is\n// therefore a capture of live source behavior, not a hand-edited fixture.\n//\n// It scores in the regime the ratchet replays: both scorers read a skill graph\n// indexed fresh from this checkout into a temporary directory, and the clock is\n// pinned to midnight UTC of the capture day, which the ledger records as its\n// scoringInstant. Requires a built dist (npm run build).\n//\n// Usage:\n//   node capture-local-native-divergence-ledger.mjs\n//   node capture-local-native-divergence-ledger.mjs --write\n//   node capture-local-native-divergence-ledger.mjs --write --summary\n\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 1. IMPORTS\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nimport { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\nimport { createHash } from 'node:crypto';\nimport { existsSync, mkdtempSync, readFileSync, rmSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { tmpdir } from 'node:os';\nimport { basename, dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\nimport { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\n\n// This script must run as a lone copy (its parity test copies only this file),\n// so it keeps its own option check instead of importing the shared helper.\nfunction rejectUnknownOptions() {\n  const scriptName = basename(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));\n  const known = new Set(['--write', '--summary']);\n  for (const token of process.argv.slice(2)) {\n    if (known.has(token)) continue;\n    process.stderr.write(`${scriptName}: unknown option: ${token}\\n`);\n    process.stderr.write(`usage: ${scriptName} [--write] [--summary]\\n`);\n    process.exit(2);\n===== capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: SCORER EVAL BASELINE CAPTURE\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n//\n// Captures the current scorer accuracy across the full corpus, the independent\n// holdout, the frozen ambiguity slice, and the named intent buckets, under the\n// reproducible filesystem projection (empty SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DB_DIR → SQLite\n// loader returns null → projection built purely from committed graph metadata).\n// The result is the ratchet baseline: the ratchet gate re-scores live under the\n// same env and holds every metric to it, so any scoring or metadata change that\n// moves a number forces a conscious re-capture rather than drifting silently.\n//\n// The *Sha256 fields pin the baseline to the exact fixtures it was captured\n// against; a fixture edit changes a hash and forces re-baselining.\n//\n// Usage:\n//   node capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs            # print JSON to stdout\n//   node capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs --write     # also write the baseline\n//   node capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs --misses    # print each failing prompt, one JSON line each\n//   SPECKIT_EVAL_BASELINE_SCORING_DATE=YYYY-MM-DD node capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs --write\n//                                                     # move the pinned scoring date\n\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 1. IMPORTS\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nimport { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\nimport { createHash } from 'node:crypto';\nimport { mkdtempSync, readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { tmpdir } from 'node:os';\nimport { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\nimport { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\nimport { rejectUnknownOptions } from './reject-unknown-options.mjs';\n\nrejectUnknownOptions(import.meta.url, ['--write', '--misses']);\n\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 2. CONSTANTS\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n===== derive-ambiguity-slice.mjs\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: Empirical Ambiguity Slice Deriver\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n//\n// The labeled corpus carries no expected-ambiguous labels, so an ambiguity\n// false-positive/negative rate cannot be computed. What CAN be measured\n// honestly is top-1 accuracy on the hardest (lowest top-2 margin) prompts: a\n// regression that only hurts contested prompts is invisible in the aggregate\n// but fails this slice.\n//\n// The slice is derived once and frozen: the top-2 fused-score margin is\n// computed for every corpus row under the reproducible filesystem projection,\n// and rows with `margin <= tau` are kept with their existing gold label. tau is\n// chosen as the tightest genuinely-contested band (a meaningful minority, well\n// inside the near-tie window), and both tau and the resulting row-id set are\n// frozen so the slice never silently re-derives on a corpus edit.\n//\n// The frozen `margin_at_capture` is advisory (it documents why a row is in the\n// slice); the gate recomputes margin live and does not trust it.\n//\n// Determinism: an empty SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DB_DIR forces the SQLite loader to\n// return null, yielding the clean filesystem projection built purely from\n// committed graph metadata — reproducible from the repo alone.\n//\n// Usage:\n//   node derive-ambiguity-slice.mjs            # print summary only\n//   node derive-ambiguity-slice.mjs --write    # (re)write ambiguity-prompts.jsonl\n\nimport { mkdtempSync, readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { tmpdir } from 'node:os';\nimport { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\nimport { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\nimport { rejectUnknownOptions } from './reject-unknown-options.mjs';\n\nrejectUnknownOptions(import.meta.url, ['--write']);\n\n// The tightest contested margin band: a meaningful minority of the corpus.\nconst TAU = 0.03;\nconst CAPTURED_AT = '2026-07-07';\n\n===== prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: Behaviour Arena Builder\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n//\n// A live turn has to run where it cannot reach anything outside its own\n// checkout, and where the advisor it measures is built the way a user gets it.\n// A clone of the branch tip in a directory git does not own gives both. The\n// clone has no remote to push to and no outer repository whose hooks or\n// credentials it could borrow, and its tracked tree is exactly the commit under\n// test, so a per-turn restore has a clean baseline to put back.\n//\n// A clone also leaves out every ignored build output. The advisor under test\n// runs from compiled JavaScript and its SQLite skill graph, so those trees are\n// copied in afterwards. Building them inside the clone instead would measure a\n// bundle nobody reviewed, and would need the network while an arena is meant to\n// be offline.\n//\n// A copy also has to look like a build. The freshness guard each CLI shim runs\n// falls back to comparing the newest watched source against the build's own\n// timestamp, and a checkout stamps every source with the clone time while the\n// copied build keeps the moment it was compiled. Left alone, that order says\n// every copied package is stale and every shim exits before running. The copied\n// build is the same commit's build, so its timestamp is set to the copy time,\n// which is what a build in this checkout would carry.\n//\n// Nothing is copied before the destination is checked. A destination inside a\n// repository would inherit that repository's hooks and ignore rules, and a\n// destination that already exists may hold another arm's log, so both are\n// refused rather than merged into.\n//\n// The copy reads the source worktree, so the one thing it must never carry\n// across is a dotenv file. A model reads and writes an arena, so a secret that\n// followed the build output in would sit where a run can print it. Every file\n// whose name begins with `.env` is skipped while copying, at every depth.\n//\n// Usage:\n//   node prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs --source <repo root> --dest <dir>\n\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 1. IMPORTS\n===== reject-unknown-options.mjs\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: Shared unknown-option guard\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n//\n// A misspelled flag must fail before any work runs: silently ignoring it\n// would run the default behavior and make the no-op look successful.\n\nimport { basename } from 'node:path';\nimport { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\n\n/**\n * Exit 2 on the first argument outside a script's own option list.\n *\n * @param {string} scriptUrl - The calling module's import.meta.url.\n * @param {string[]} knownOptions - Every argument the caller accepts.\n */\nexport function rejectUnknownOptions(scriptUrl, knownOptions) {\n  const scriptName = basename(fileURLToPath(scriptUrl));\n  const known = new Set(knownOptions);\n  for (const token of process.argv.slice(2)) {\n    if (known.has(token)) continue;\n    process.stderr.write(`${scriptName}: unknown option: ${token}\\n`);\n    process.stderr.write(`usage: ${scriptName} ${knownOptions.map((option) => `[${option}]`).join(' ')}\\n`);\n    process.exit(2);\n  }\n}\n===== score-outcome-rerank.mjs\n#!/usr/bin/env node\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// COMPONENT: OUTCOME-WEIGHTED RERANK ROUTING-ACCURACY EVAL DRIVER\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n//\n// Measures whether the outcome-weighted re-rank improves routing accuracy over\n// the similarity-only baseline, reported as MRR (mean reciprocal rank of the\n// gold skill) AND right-skill@3 — not @1 alone. A near-tie reorder mainly moves\n// a skill between adjacent ranks, so @1 is a coarse, noisy lens; MRR and @3 see\n// the fuller ranking shift and are the gate this packet reads.\n//\n// The flag governs `outcomeWeightedRerank` (lib/scorer/outcome-weighted-rerank),\n// which blends live fused similarity with an observed-outcome reliability term\n// through the shared Beta posterior. This eval scores the rerank's full ordering\n// against the similarity-only ordering it reorders.\n//\n// Honest signal, not leakage: the reliability fold is built from a TRAIN split\n// of the labeled corpus (skill marked correct => success, wrong => failure),\n// then accuracy is measured ONLY on a disjoint HELD-OUT split. A flag that\n// merely memorised the labels would not transfer across the split.\n//\n// READ-ONLY: scores against the live skill-graph.sqlite via a read path; never\n// mutates any database.\n\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 1. IMPORTS\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nimport { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\nimport { dirname, join } from 'node:path';\n\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 2. CONSTANTS\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nconst SCRIPT_DIR = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));\n// routing-accuracy -> scripts -> mcp_server\nconst MCP_SERVER = join(SCRIPT_DIR, '..', '..');\nconst REPO_ROOT = join(MCP_SERVER, '..', '..', '..', '..');\nconst CORPUS = join(SCRIPT_DIR, 'labeled-prompts.jsonl');\n\n===== worktree-restore.mjs\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: Worktree restore\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n//\n// A measured turn runs inside a checkout someone else may be working in, so the\n// runner has to put back exactly what the turn changed and nothing else. The\n// snapshot is the baseline: any path that was already dirty when the turn began\n// belongs to an earlier edit and is never touched, even when the turn adds that\n// path to the index on top of the edit.\n//\n// Restoring from the index is not enough. A staged new file is in the index, so\n// restoring from the index keeps it, while only HEAD is the state the turn\n// started from. A path HEAD knows is checked out from HEAD, and a path HEAD does\n// not know is dropped from the index and deleted from the worktree. A second\n// snapshot after the restore reports what is still different, so the caller can\n// fail the run instead of claiming a clean worktree.\n\nimport { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\nimport { rmSync } from 'node:fs';\nimport { resolve } from 'node:path';\n\n// A filesystem monitor that decides a path is unchanged makes a status snapshot\n// lie, and the restore trusts that snapshot.\nconst GIT_PREFIX = ['-c', 'core.fsmonitor=false'];\n\nfunction git(cwd, args) {\n  const result = spawnSync('git', [...GIT_PREFIX, ...args], { cwd, encoding: 'utf8' });\n  if (result.error) throw result.error;\n  return result;\n}\n\nfunction trimTrailingSlash(path) {\n  return path.endsWith('/') ? path.slice(0, -1) : path;\n}\n\n/**\n * Read the working tree state of one checkout.\n *\n * Porcelain names each path relative to the checkout, and a rename names both\n * the old and the new path. Both sides are separate keys because both have to\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"for f in build-holdout.mjs capture-local-native-divergence-ledger.mjs capture-scorer-eval-baseline.mjs derive-ambiguity-slice.mjs prepare-behaviour-arena.mjs reject-unknown-options.mjs score-outcome-rerank.mjs worktree-restore.mjs; do echo \\\"===== $f\\\"; sed -n '1,40p' \\\".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/$f\\\"; done\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research\"}"},"time":{"start":1794560851505,"end":1794560852652}}}}
